&lt;/span>
&lt;/h2>
&lt;p>&lt;strong>Part 18 of the &lt;em>Learning Quantum Physics&lt;/em> series.&lt;/strong>&lt;/p>
&lt;p>Most introductions to Bell&amp;rsquo;s inequality jump straight to jargon: &lt;em>entanglement&lt;/em>, &lt;em>hidden variables&lt;/em>, &lt;em>locality&lt;/em>, &lt;em>realism&lt;/em>. The result sounds mysterious before you even know what the experiment looks like.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>That is backwards.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>Bell&amp;rsquo;s theorem is really an answer to one plain question:&lt;/p>
&lt;blockquote>
&lt;p>&lt;strong>Can the correlations we see in quantum experiments be explained by particles carrying local, predetermined answers to every possible measurement?&lt;/strong>&lt;/p>&lt;/blockquote>
&lt;p>This article builds that question from scratch — no prior quantum mechanics assumed.&lt;/p></description></item></channel></rss>